    "['set protocols bfd interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface ae1.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface ae1.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the BFD settings:\\n\\n##\\ninterface ae1.0\\n bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper `set protocols bfd interface ae1.0` command enables BFD on the interface\\n- The `minimum-interval 10` translates to both the transmit interval and minimum receive interval of 10 ms in Cisco (specified as `interval 10 min_rx 10`)\\n- The `multiplier 3` remains the same in Cisco, indicating 3 missed packets before declaring the neighbor down\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, the interface naming might differ as \"ae1.0\" is a Juniper-style name. Cisco might use something like \"Bundle-Ethernet1\" or \"Port-channel1\" depending on the platform. The actual interface name would need to match Cisco\\'s naming convention for the aggregate interface.', 'trans_template': 'interface ae1.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the BFD settings:\\n\\n##\\ninterface ae1.0\\n bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper `set protocols bfd interface ae1.0` command enables BFD on the interface\\n- The `minimum-interval 10` translates to both the transmit interval and minimum receive interval of 10 ms in Cisco (specified as `interval 10 min_rx 10`)\\n- The `multiplier 3` remains the same in Cisco, indicating 3 missed packets before declaring the neighbor down\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, the interface naming might differ as \"ae1.0\" is a Juniper-style name. Cisco might use something like \"Bundle-Ethernet1\" or \"Port-channel1\" depending on the platform. The actual interface name would need to match Cisco\\'s naming convention for the aggregate interface.', 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3'}]]": 1,
    "['minimum-interval [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'parent_command': ['interface ae1.0'], 'root': 'interface ae1.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BFD configuration:\\n\\n##\\nbfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper `minimum-interval` parameter translates to both the transmit interval and minimum receive interval in Cisco (hence `interval 10 min_rx 10`)\\n- While not in the specific command you asked to translate, I included the `multiplier 3` from the original Juniper configuration since BFD requires both interval and multiplier values to function properly in Cisco\\n- The interface specification isn't needed in the Cisco BFD command line as it's configured under the interface context in Cisco implementations\", 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3'}]]": 1,
    "['multiplier [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3', 'parent_command': ['interface ae1.0'], 'root': 'interface ae1.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BFD multiplier configuration:\\n\\n##\\nbfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, the BFD configuration is typically done under the interface context. The complete Cisco equivalent would include both the interval and multiplier settings (unlike Juniper where they\\'re separate commands). The \"interval\" is the desired transmit interval, \"min_rx\" is the required receive interval (set to match), and \"multiplier\" is the same as in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'bfd interval 10 min_rx 10 multiplier 3'}]]": 1
